Let's write Rust:

```rust
use std::cmp::Ordering;

/// Partitions a slice around its first element.
/// Returns a new vector with elements <= pivot first, then pivot, then elements > pivot.
/// This is a single quicksort partition step.
pub fn partition_first<T: Ord + Copy>(a: &[T]) -> Vec<T> {
    if a.len() <= 1 {
        return a.to_vec();
    }
    let pivot = a[0];
    let mut less = Vec::with_capacity(a.len());
    let mut greater = Vec::with_capacity(a.len());
    for &x in &a[1..] {
        if x <= pivot {
            less.push(x);
        } else {
            greater.push(x);
        }
    }
    less.push(pivot);
    less.extend(greater);
    less
}
```

But this still uses two vectors and then concatenates. Could do one vector and insert pivot? But we need pivot in middle. We can build result: first push all <= pivot, then pivot, then all > pivot. But we don't know which are > until we see them. We can do two passes or collect greater separately. To avoid two vectors, we can do: create result with capacity, first pass push <= pivot, then push pivot, then second pass push > pivot. That's two passes but one result vector. Or we can do one pass and store greater in a temporary, then append. That's what above does. It's fine.

