Better: Use standard Lomuto with pivot at end. Or use Hoare partition. Let's write a correct in-place partition that returns pivot index.

Standard Lomuto partition (pivot = last element):
```rust
fn partition<T: Ord>(a: &mut [T], low: usize, high: usize) -> usize {
    let pivot = a[high];
    let mut i = low;
    for j in low..high {
        if a[j] <= pivot {
            a.swap(i, j);
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    a.swap(i, high);
    i
}
```
This places pivot at i, elements <= pivot left, > pivot right. But original uses first element as pivot. We can adapt: swap first element with last, then use Lomuto, then swap back? Or just use first element as pivot and do Hoare.

Hoare partition with first element as pivot:
```rust
fn partition<T: Ord>(a: &mut [T]) -> usize {
    let pivot = a[0];
    let mut i = 0;
    let mut j = a.len();
    loop {
        i += 1;
        while a[i] < pivot { i += 1; }
        j -= 1;
        while a[j] > pivot { j -= 1; }
        if i >= j { return j; }
        a.swap(i, j);
    }
}
```
This is Hoare partition, but it doesn't place pivot exactly in middle; it returns split point. It's efficient and handles duplicates better. But it's more complex.

